1
00:00:00,990 --> 00:00:04,050
Shared VPC lets you create one central VPC,

2
00:00:04,230 --> 00:00:05,590
we call it the host project,

3
00:00:05,630 --> 00:00:09,380
and then you can attach this with multiple service projects.

4
00:00:09,590 --> 00:00:14,510
For example, you create a one shared VPC that you call it as a host project,

5
00:00:14,569 --> 00:00:18,069
and inside that project you define all your subnets,

6
00:00:18,270 --> 00:00:19,780
routes, etc.

7
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:25,670
And to this specific shared VPC, you attach the multiple service project.

8
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:28,870
In short, your networking team manages the network,

9
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:32,025
and your application team uses the network.

10
00:00:32,165 --> 00:00:37,685
This is the same model used by the Fortune 500 companies because it

11
00:00:37,685 --> 00:00:41,194
gives them the central control of IP address ranges.

12
00:00:41,325 --> 00:00:44,645
They can effectively manage their consistent firewalls,

13
00:00:44,805 --> 00:00:49,525
no duplicate VPC, no IP conflicts, and no unnecessary public access.

14
00:00:49,645 --> 00:00:50,875
And most importantly,

15
00:00:51,255 --> 00:00:56,485
shared VPC is a zero‑trust friendly model because service projects cannot

16
00:00:56,485 --> 00:01:00,310
modify the network settings as they cannot touch it.

17
00:01:00,459 --> 00:01:03,979
There is another important pattern, that is VPC peering.

18
00:01:04,099 --> 00:01:08,339
If shared VPC is like joining multiple rooms inside the same house,

19
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:13,559
VPC peering is like connecting two different houses with a private walkway.

20
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:18,739
It is basically a private connection between the two independent VPCs.

21
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:22,339
Here are some of the common scenarios where you're going to use the

22
00:01:22,339 --> 00:01:26,114
peering. When two departments build network independently,

23
00:01:26,235 --> 00:01:29,450
but now they want to integrate and talk to each other,

24
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:31,114
that's where you can use peering.

25
00:01:31,235 --> 00:01:34,315
You might have a one partner team and a one vendor team,

26
00:01:34,425 --> 00:01:35,754
and they both want to talk.

27
00:01:35,875 --> 00:01:38,794
That's where we can also use the VPC peering.

28
00:01:38,875 --> 00:01:42,794
Remember one thing, VPC peering is non‑transitive in nature.

29
00:01:42,955 --> 00:01:44,714
Everything comes with pros and cons,

30
00:01:44,834 --> 00:01:48,235
so peering also brings in a lot of pros like low latency,

31
00:01:48,434 --> 00:01:50,915
fully private, and no shared VPC requirement.

32
00:01:51,035 --> 00:01:55,690
On the other hand, it also comes with little bit cons that once you grow,

33
00:01:55,809 --> 00:01:58,319
the routing complexity grows with the scale,

34
00:01:58,449 --> 00:02:00,449
and that is a little difficult to manage.

35
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:04,279
There is another concept, PSC, that is Private Service Connect.

36
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:07,330
PSC allows you to access Google services,

37
00:02:07,610 --> 00:02:08,639
partner services,

38
00:02:08,889 --> 00:02:14,110
or even your own service privately without exposing traffic to the internet.

39
00:02:14,210 --> 00:02:15,690
Let's understand the scenario one.

40
00:02:15,779 --> 00:02:18,690
Instead of calling the Google service over the internet,

41
00:02:18,809 --> 00:02:20,850
you create a PSC endpoint.

42
00:02:21,059 --> 00:02:25,684
Now traffic stays inside the Google's backbone, no public IP needed.

43
00:02:25,794 --> 00:02:26,744
Another scenario,

44
00:02:27,024 --> 00:02:30,945
imagine you have a service in one project and another

45
00:02:30,945 --> 00:02:33,735
team in another project need the access.

46
00:02:33,865 --> 00:02:37,544
Instead of opening the firewall rules or peering networks,

47
00:02:37,695 --> 00:02:43,065
you publish the service using the PSC, and they connect via private endpoint.

48
00:02:43,195 --> 00:02:46,645
This gives you the isolation, traffic policy control,

49
00:02:46,664 --> 00:02:49,225
and no overlapping IP issues.
